  "summary": "Hinoki-grid sliding screens papered in washi — architecture turned into soft light.",
  "description": "Shoji are sliding panels of fine cedar or hinoki lattice (kumiko) — joined without nails — glued over with washi paper so rooms receive sculpted, shadowless light. The grammar scales from a single window to whole facades; kumiko work itself became a virtuoso craft of asanoha stars and interlocking geometry. Shoji discipline — precise joinery, translucent planes, modularity — shaped Japanese modernism (Bruno Taut's praise of Katsura) and Western light-architecture, from Frank Lloyd Wright screens to contemporary shoji-inspired facades.",
